course: dictionary
lesson: 13331
title: The gullet, from mouth to stomach
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A gullet is a tube in your body. When you swallow, food goes from your mouth down the gullet into your stomach. Scientists also call it the oesophagus.
explain: Read the words around it to work out what it means. «The bird swallowed the worm, and it slid down its gullet.» The worm went down, from the mouth towards the stomach, so the gullet must be the way food travels.
explain: Gullet can also mean the throat, the part at the back of your mouth that you swallow through. «The frog's gullet swelled as it gulped the fly.» Here the throat is the part that swelled. Both meanings are about swallowing.
fact: The gullet is the tube that food goes down from the mouth to the stomach.
fact: Food is moved down the gullet by muscles that squeeze in turn, one part after another.
fact: The gullet is also called the oesophagus, a science word for the same tube.
fact: Gullet can also mean the throat.
fact: In Kipling's story «How the Whale got his Throat», a sailor fixes a grating in the Whale's throat, so it can only swallow very small fish. This is a made-up story, not real science.
exercise: «Food travels down the gullet to the stomach.» What is the gullet? | The part of the brain that thinks | A bone in the leg | A kind of small fish | *The tube food goes down from the mouth to the stomach
exercise: Tomas says the gullet is his stomach. Who is right? | Tomas is right, the gullet is the stomach itself | *Tomas is wrong: the gullet is the tube that leads to the stomach | Tomas, because the stomach and the gullet are the same | Neither, the gullet is in the nose
exercise: In Kipling's story, «The sailor fixed a grating in the Whale's throat.» What did the sailor fix? | A lamp to light up the sea | A hole in the boat | A new song for the whale | *A metal grid, so only small fish could pass
test: «The bird swallowed the worm, and it slid down its gullet.» What does gullet mean here? | The sea where the bird lives | A bird's beak | The tail of the worm | *The tube from the mouth to the stomach
test: How does food move down the gullet? | A tiny motor pushes it | The wind blows it into the stomach | *Muscles squeeze it along, one part after another | It falls down by itself, like a stone
exam: Which statement about the Whale's throat in Kipling's story is true? | *The story is made up, so it is not real science | The whale really did swallow a boat | Real scientists have shown that a grating in a whale's throat stops it eating big fish | The sailor was a real scientist who studied whales
